= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a vector $x$, its <isotropy group> is the <stabilizer subgroup>
$$
\boxed{G_x=\{g\in G:R_G(g)x=x\}.}
$$
For a <subgroup> $H\le G$, its <fixed-point subspace of a group action> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Fix}(H)=\{x\in\mathbb R^n:R_G(h)x=x\text{ for every }h\in H\}.}
$$
It is a <linear subspace>, the intersection of the <kernels> of $R_G(h)-I$. The <subgroup> fixes a particular vector pointwise; the <fixed-point subspace of a group action> consists of all vectors it fixes, and need not be one-dimensional.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Suppose $f$ is sufficiently smooth and <equivariant>, $f(0,\mu)=0$, and the <group representation> on the critical space is <absolutely irreducible>. Its <linearization> is then $D_xf(0,\mu)=a(\mu)I$. Assume $a(0)=0$ and $a'(0)\ne0$. The <equivariant branching lemma> states that **each isotropy <subgroup> $H$ with $\dim\operatorname{Fix}(H)=1$ has a local nontrivial branch of <equilibria> with that isotropy**, apart from the common origin. Conjugate isotropy <subgroups> give symmetry-related branches, so branch types are counted up to conjugacy.

To see the scalar mechanism, write the <fixed-point subspace of a group action> as $\mathbb Re$ and restrict $f(se,\mu)=e\,h(s,\mu)$. <Equivariance> makes the <fixed-point subspace of a group action> invariant. Since $h(0,\mu)=0$, write $h=s\,k(s,\mu)$; $k(0,0)=0$ and $k_\mu(0,0)=a'(0)\ne0$. The <implicit function theorem> solves $k(s,\mu(s))=0$ near zero. For small nonzero $s$, the <stabilizer subgroup> is exactly that of $e$. A <normalizer> element reversing $e$ makes $h$ odd and $\mu(s)$ even; the generic branch is then a <pitchfork bifurcation>. The lemma ensures existence, not <dynamical stability>, and does not exclude further branches of smaller isotropy.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Conjugating the coordinate <reflection> by the rotations supplies <reflections> in the other two coordinates. The quarter-turns, together with those <reflections>, also supply coordinate transpositions. Consequently the represented <group> consists of all signed permutation <matrices>, the full <symmetry group of a cube> of order $2^3\cdot3!=48$.

Let $C$ be any real or complex <matrix> commuting with the <group representation>. Commutation with the three independent coordinate <reflections> forces all off-diagonal entries of $C$ to vanish. Commutation with the quarter-turn about the first axis equates its second and third diagonal entries, and the quarter-turn about the second axis equates its first and third entries. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{End}_\Gamma(\mathbb C^3)=\mathbb C I.}
$$
For completeness, if the complexified <group representation> had a proper invariant subspace, its <orthogonal complement> would be invariant too, since the generating <matrices> are unitary. The <orthogonal projection> onto that subspace would be a nonscalar commuting <matrix>, contradicting the calculation. Thus the complexification is <irreducible>, proving **<absolute irreducibility>** of the real <group representation>.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Choose three representative vectors, with nonzero coordinate scale suppressed. Their <stabilizer subgroups> and <fixed-point subspaces of a group action> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{vector}&\text{stabilizer}&\text{order}&\text{fixed space}\\\hline
(1,0,0)&\langle r_x,\kappa_y,\kappa_z\rangle\cong D_4&8&\mathbb R(1,0,0)\\
(1,1,0)&\langle\kappa_z,\kappa_xr_z\rangle\cong C_2\times C_2&4&\mathbb R(1,1,0)\\
(1,1,1)&\langle\kappa_xr_z,\kappa_yr_x\rangle\cong S_3&6&\mathbb R(1,1,1).
\end{array}
$$
Here $\kappa_xr_z$ exchanges the first two coordinates and $\kappa_yr_x$ exchanges the last two. On the first line the <stabilizer subgroup> is every signed permutation of the two zero coordinates; fixing it forces those coordinates to vanish. On the second line it exchanges the equal coordinates and independently reflects the zero coordinate, forcing $x=y,z=0$. On the third line all coordinate permutations fix the vector, forcing $x=y=z$.

The three <subgroups> have different orders and hence cannot be conjugate. Their <group orbits> contain respectively six face-axis, twelve edge-axis and eight vertex-axis directions. Each <fixed-point subspace of a group action> is one-dimensional, so the <equivariant branching lemma> guarantees **three distinct symmetry types of bifurcating <equilibria>**. The <axial isotropy types of the full cube symmetry group> are precisely these: a vector with several distinct nonzero absolute coordinate values has a separate freely variable coordinate block for each such value in the <fixed-point subspace of a group action> of its <stabilizer subgroup>, giving dimension greater than one.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Coordinate <reflection> in $x_i$ requires the $i$th component to be odd in $x_i$, and <reflections> in the other coordinates require it to be even in those coordinates. Thus no constant or quadratic terms are permitted. The only cubic monomials in component $i$ are $x_i^3$ and $x_ix_j^2$ for $j\ne i$. Coordinate permutations equate their coefficients in all components. After using the transverse linear <eigenvalue> as parameter, the <normal form> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\dot x&=\mu x+a x^3+b x(y^2+z^2)+\cdots,\\
\dot y&=\mu y+a y^3+b y(z^2+x^2)+\cdots,\\
\dot z&=\mu z+a z^3+b z(x^2+y^2)+\cdots.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The remainder starts at fifth spatial order, together with parameter-dependent corrections such as $\mu|x|^3$. This is the <cubic equivariants of the full cube symmetry group> form. On a branch with $m=1,2,3$ equal nonzero absolute coordinates $s$, respectively,
$$
\mu+[a+(m-1)b]s^2=0.
$$
Hence the three leading branch amplitudes are $s^2=-\mu/a$, $-\mu/(a+b)$ and $-\mu/(a+2b)$. Their generic nonzero denominators determine on which side the branches occur; existence alone does not make them stable.

