= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $g(x)=x^2+x^3/3$. <Equilibria> satisfy $\mu^2=g(x)$, and their scalar linear <eigenvalue> is
$$
F_x=-x(x+2).
$$
It vanishes only at $x=0$ or $x=-2$. Substitution in the <equilibrium> relation gives the three nonhyperbolic parameter-state points
$$
\boxed{(\mu,x)=(0,0),\quad(2/\sqrt3,-2),\quad(-2/\sqrt3,-2).}
$$
The branches with $x<-2$ and $x>0$ are stable, while $-2<x<0$ is unstable. For $0<|\mu|<2/\sqrt3$ there are three <equilibria>; beyond the two outer <saddle-node bifurcations> there is just the positive stable one. At $\mu=0$ there is also the distinct stable <equilibrium> $x=-3$.

Near the crossing, use the smooth coordinate $y=x\sqrt{1+x/3}$, whose derivative is positive near zero. The equation becomes $\dot y=p(y)(\mu^2-y^2)$ with $p>0$. A positive time rescaling removes $p$, and the parameter-dependent state shift $u=y-\mu$ gives
$$
\frac{du}{d\tau}=-2\mu u-u^2.
$$
Thus **the crossing at $\mu=0$ is a <transcritical bifurcation>**, with parameter $-2\mu$. The two smooth branches $y=\pm\mu$ exchange <dynamical stability>; labeling the stable branch by $|\mu|$ instead would obscure that exchange. The <parameter-dependent coordinate shift in a transcritical bifurcation> explains why the original coordinate need not display a branch identically equal to zero.

\Image[paper-60-scalar-unfoldings.png]
{title=Scalar equilibrium diagrams before and after positive and negative unfolding, with stable branches solid and unstable branches dashed}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write $h=\mu^2+\varepsilon$. The entire unfolded diagram is obtained from $g(x)=h$, with the same <dynamical stability> sign $-x(x+2)$ as before. A <saddle-node bifurcation> at $x=0$ requires $h=0$, while a <saddle-node bifurcation> at $x=-2$ requires $h=4/3$.

For $0<\varepsilon<4/3$, **the crossing separates into a positive stable branch and a negative unstable branch**, with no <saddle-node bifurcation> near $\mu=0$. Three <equilibria> exist for $|\mu|<\sqrt{4/3-\varepsilon}$, and the two negative branches annihilate at the outer <saddle-node bifurcations> $\mu=\pm\sqrt{4/3-\varepsilon}$. Outside that interval only the positive stable <equilibrium> remains. If $\varepsilon>4/3$, the diagram has just that one branch everywhere; equality is the limiting <saddle-node bifurcation> contact at $\mu=0,x=-2$.

For $\varepsilon<0$, let $d=-\varepsilon>0$. **Two inner <saddle-node bifurcations> occur at $\mu=\pm\sqrt d,x=0$**, and two outer <saddle-node bifurcations> at $\mu=\pm\sqrt{4/3+d},x=-2$. For $|\mu|<\sqrt d$ there is a single stable <equilibrium> below $-3$; for $\sqrt d<|\mu|<\sqrt{4/3+d}$ there are three <equilibria> with stable–unstable–stable ordering; outside the outer <saddle-node bifurcations> only the positive stable <equilibrium> remains. The preceding figure shows representative small unfoldings. In particular this unrestricted perturbation destroys the exact <transcritical bifurcation> crossing.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $w=\dot v$. The planar <vector field> and its <equilibrium> <eigenvalue> data are
$$
\dot v=w,\qquad\dot w=-\lambda v+2\varepsilon v^2+v^3+(\kappa-v^2)w,
$$
$$
v_0=0,\quad v_\pm=-\varepsilon\pm\sqrt{\varepsilon^2+\lambda},\qquad
\operatorname{tr}J=\kappa-v_*^2,\quad\det J=\lambda-4\varepsilon v_*-3v_*^2.
$$
For a nonzero <equilibrium>, use $\lambda=v_*^2+2\varepsilon v_*$ to rewrite the <determinant> as $-2v_*(v_*+\varepsilon)$. These formulas determine the local bifurcations of the <imperfect soft Duffing-van der Pol oscillator> directly.

When $\varepsilon=0$, the origin is a <saddle equilibrium> for $\lambda<0$ and has <determinant> $\lambda$ for $\lambda>0$; the two <equilibria> $\pm\sqrt\lambda$ are <saddle equilibria>. They meet the origin in a symmetry-protected <pitchfork bifurcation> at $\lambda=0$ for $\kappa\ne0$. At $\kappa=0,\lambda>0$ the origin undergoes a <supercritical Hopf bifurcation>. To determine criticality, take $v=r\cos(\sqrt\lambda t)+\cdots$: averaging $\dot H=(\kappa-v^2)w^2$ gives $\dot r=\kappa r/2-r^3/8+\cdots$, so the stable small <limit cycle> has $r^2=4\kappa+\cdots$.

The <potential energy> of the conservative part and the exact <energy> balance are
$$
H=\frac12w^2+\frac12\lambda v^2-\frac23\varepsilon v^3-\frac14v^4,
\qquad\dot H=(\kappa-v^2)w^2.
$$
At zero imperfection the stable <limit cycle> grows to a <heteroclinic cycle> connecting the two outer <saddle equilibria>. Near the double-zero point its leading connection curve can be calculated, rather than inferred just from symmetry. In the <Hamiltonian system> limit the <heteroclinic orbit> is $v=\sqrt\lambda\tanh(\sqrt{\lambda/2}\,t)$, and
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}w^2dt=\frac{4\lambda^{3/2}}{3\sqrt2},\qquad
\frac{\int v^2w^2dt}{\int w^2dt}=\frac\lambda5.
$$
The weak-damping persistence condition is therefore $\kappa=\lambda/5+o(\lambda)$, not an exact formula at order-one parameters.

Now take $\varepsilon>0$; negative imperfection reflects the phase portrait. The <pitchfork bifurcation> unfolds into **a <transcritical bifurcation> crossing at $\lambda=0$ and a <saddle-node bifurcation> at $\lambda=-\varepsilon^2$**. The latter occurs at $v=-\varepsilon$. Between those values the origin and $v_-$ are <saddle equilibria>, while $v_+$ lies in $(-\varepsilon,0)$ and is a sink or source according to the <trace>. At $\lambda=0$, $v_+=\lambda/(2\varepsilon)+\cdots$ exchanges its <saddle equilibrium>/non-saddle character with the origin. The double-zero points are now at $(\lambda,\kappa)=(0,0)$ and $(-\varepsilon^2,\varepsilon^2)$.

The original <Hopf bifurcation> line $\kappa=0,\lambda>0$ remains, still supercritical. A second <Hopf bifurcation> curve occurs on the intermediate nonzero <equilibrium>:
$$
\boxed{\lambda=q^2+2\varepsilon q,\quad\kappa=q^2,\quad-\varepsilon<q<0.}
$$
It is important not to assign the same criticality to its entire length. Write $s=\sqrt{\varepsilon^2+\lambda}$, so $q=-\varepsilon+s$. Around that center the restoring quadratic coefficient is $g_2=-\varepsilon+3s$, the damping linear coefficient is $f_1=2(\varepsilon-s)$, and $\omega^2=2s(\varepsilon-s)$. The <Hopf criticality for an asymmetric Lienard center> calculation gives cubic amplitude coefficient
$$
\frac18\left(-1+\frac{f_1g_2}{\omega^2}\right)=\frac18(2-\varepsilon/s).
$$
Hence it is supercritical for $s<\varepsilon/2$ and subcritical for $s>\varepsilon/2$, with a <generalized Hopf bifurcation> point at $\lambda=-3\varepsilon^2/4,\kappa=\varepsilon^2/4$. Higher-order terms are needed exactly there. For small imperfection the leading quintic coefficient is positive: writing $q_1=v-q$ and using the coordinate that normalizes the <potential energy>, $y=q_1\sqrt{1-aq_1-bq_1^2}$, with $a=2g_2/(3\omega^2)$ and $b=1/(2\omega^2)$, the coefficient of $y^4$ in $(f_1q_1-q_1^2)dq_1/dy$ at vanishing cubic coefficient is $10b/3>0$. Weak-damping averaging therefore supplies the nearby <saddle-node bifurcation> of stable and unstable <limit cycles> in the <generalized Hopf bifurcation> unfolding. None of these small $O(\varepsilon^2)$ features intersects either requested parameter line.

Globally, the two <saddle equilibrium> barriers are no longer equal. Their leading <energy> difference is $H(v_+,0)-H(v_-,0)=-4\varepsilon\lambda^{3/2}/3+\cdots$. The two one-way connections consequently separate; matching their <energy> differences with the connection integral gives
$$
\kappa=\lambda/5-\sqrt2\,\varepsilon+\cdots\quad(v_-\to v_+),\qquad
\kappa=\lambda/5+\sqrt2\,\varepsilon+\cdots\quad(v_+\to v_-).
$$
These <weak-damping heteroclinic splitting of a tilted quartic oscillator> formulas apply for small $\lambda$ with $|\varepsilon|\ll\sqrt\lambda$. Between the separated connection curves, the central stable <limit cycle> terminates in a <homoclinic orbit> to the lower-barrier <saddle equilibrium>, $v_+$ for positive imperfection. The simultaneous symmetric heteroclinic-cycle destruction is therefore replaced by two basin-changing one-way connections and a distinct <homoclinic orbit> loss of the <limit cycle>. Its period diverges at that loss.

Along $\kappa+\lambda=1$, decrease $\lambda$ from above one. The sequence is **<supercritical Hopf bifurcation> at $\lambda=1$; first <heteroclinic orbit>; <homoclinic orbit> loss of the stable <limit cycle>; second <heteroclinic orbit>; <transcritical bifurcation> crossing at zero; <saddle-node bifurcation> at $-\varepsilon^2$**. The connection locations are global, not obtained by treating the Melnikov approximation as exact. For illustration, direct stable/unstable manifold matching at $\varepsilon=0.04$ places them at approximately $\lambda=0.882,0.858,0.783$, respectively. The diagram below gives the qualitative ordering without prescribing those example values for arbitrary imperfection.

Along $\kappa+\lambda=-1$, the sequence is simply **<transcritical bifurcation> crossing at zero, followed by <saddle-node bifurcation> at $-\varepsilon^2$**. Before the latter, $\kappa<0$ in the relevant region, so $H$ decreases strictly on nonstationary trajectories and there is no <periodic orbit> or <saddle equilibrium> loop. After it only a <saddle equilibrium> remains, which cannot be enclosed by a <periodic orbit> by the <Poincare-index obstruction to a periodic orbit>. The origin does not undergo a <Hopf bifurcation> at $\lambda=-1$, since its <determinant> there is negative.

\Image[paper-60-oscillator-sequences.png]
{title=Bifurcation sequences along the two diagonal parameter lines after a small positive reflection-symmetry imperfection; arrows indicate decreasing lambda}

= 3
{parent=Paper 60}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $s=\alpha^2+\pi^2$ and use modes satisfying all <boundary conditions>,
$$
\psi=a(t)\sin\alpha x\sin\pi z,\qquad
\theta=b(t)\cos\alpha x\sin\pi z+c(t)\sin2\pi z+\cdots.
$$
The quadratic <temperature> distortion $c$ is retained for the saturation calculation; it does not enter the linear onset. Substitution of the primary modes gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}-\sigma(s+Q\pi^2/s)&\sigma R\alpha/s\\\alpha&-s\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}a\\b\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The <determinant> vanishes when
$$
\boxed{R_c(Q,\alpha)=\frac{s(s^2+Q\pi^2)}{\alpha^2}.}
$$
The <trace> is $-[s+\sigma(s+Q\pi^2/s)]<0$. Thus no conjugate <eigenvalues> can cross the imaginary axis away from zero, and **the initial instability is stationary, not oscillatory**. Indeed for $R\ge0$ the <discriminant> is $[\sigma(s+Q\pi^2/s)-s]^2+4\sigma R\alpha^2/s>0$. This is <quasistatic vertical-field magnetoconvection>: there is no independent magnetic evolution mode that could produce a magnetic-thermal <Hopf bifurcation> instability.

For a fixed box the other allowed horizontal <wavenumbers> are $n\pi/L$, and the actual first threshold is the minimum of their corresponding $R_c$ values. Vertical index $m$ replaces $\pi^2$ by $m^2\pi^2$ and strictly increases the threshold at fixed horizontal <wavenumber>, so $m=1$ is selected. The displayed formula describes the prescribed fundamental roll; part (b) optimizes its width.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $x=\alpha^2$ and $p=\pi^2$. Then
$$
R_c=\frac{(x+p)^3+Qp(x+p)}x,\qquad
\frac{dR_c}{dx}=\frac{(x+p)^2(2x-p)-Qp^2}{x^2}.
$$
The minimizing condition is
$$
2x^3+3px^2=p^3+Qp^2.
$$
Its left side increases strictly for $x>0$ and the neutral curve diverges at both ends, so there is a unique global minimum. For large $Q$, $x\to\infty$ and the leading balance is $2x^3\sim Qp^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\alpha\sim(\pi^4/2)^{1/6}Q^{1/6}.}
$$
This is the <strong-field wavenumber selection in magnetoconvection> scaling. In a box of fixed width one compares the two admissible horizontal <wavenumbers> bracketing the continuum optimum.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The primary vorticity <Jacobian determinant> vanishes because $\nabla^2\psi=-s\psi$. The quadratic <temperature> <Jacobian determinant> is
$$
J(\psi,b\cos\alpha x\sin\pi z)=\frac{\alpha\pi ab}{2}\sin2\pi z.
$$
The mean <temperature> correction therefore obeys $\dot c=-4\pi^2c-\alpha\pi ab/2$. Its <Jacobian determinant> with the primary <streamfunction> is
$$
J(\psi,c\sin2\pi z)=\alpha\pi ac\cos\alpha x(\sin3\pi z-\sin\pi z).
$$
Projecting on the retained primary modes gives, with $D=s+Q\pi^2/s$,
$$
\dot a=-\sigma Da+\sigma R\alpha b/s,\quad
\dot b=-sb+\alpha a+\alpha\pi ac,\quad
\dot c=-4\pi^2c-\alpha\pi ab/2.
$$
For a nonzero steady roll, $b=(s^2+Q\pi^2)a/(R\alpha)$ and $c=-\alpha ab/(8\pi)$. Substitution in the second equation gives, within this truncation,
$$
\boxed{a^2=\frac{8(R-R_c)}{s^2+Q\pi^2},\qquad c=-\frac{R-R_c}{\pi R}.}
$$
The positive denominator ensures branches only on the supercritical side for every $Q\ge0$. To verify <dynamical stability> as well as existence, the critical right <eigenvector> is $(1,\alpha/s)$; normalizing the left <eigenvector> gives linear <eigenvalue> derivative
$$
K=\frac{\sigma\alpha^2}{s(s+\sigma D)}>0.
$$
Quadratic slaving gives $c=-\alpha^2a^2/(8\pi s)$, and projection of its feedback gives the cubic amplitude equation
$$
\boxed{\dot a=K\left[(R-R_c)a-\frac{s^2+Q\pi^2}{8}a^3\right]+\cdots.}
$$
Its nonzero branches are stable in the center direction, while all other <eigenvalues> remain negative at a simple onset. <Reflection> about the mid-width sends $a\to-a$, so the bifurcation is **a supercritical <pitchfork bifurcation> for all $Q$**, in the generic case of a single critical roll mode.

The <cubic saturation of quasistatic magnetoconvection> result is identical in <Fourier truncation> and perturbation theory at the orders needed here. At quadratic order the only forced mode is precisely the retained horizontally uniform $\sin2\pi z$ <temperature> mode. At cubic order its feedback projects on the primary mode with the same coefficient. The generated third vertical harmonic is orthogonal to that primary projection and contributes only to higher-order corrections. Thus both methods give the same linear threshold and cubic solvability condition; the truncated finite-amplitude branch is not an exact solution of the full PDE at all amplitudes.

= 4
{parent=Paper 60}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

After one iteration $y_n\in\{1,-1\}$, so work on that invariant sign sheet. For $x_n\ne0$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
z_{n+1}&=-x_{n+1}y_{n+1}
=\mu-A\operatorname{sgn}(y_n)\operatorname{sgn}(x_n)|x_n|^\delta\\
&=\boxed{\mu+A\operatorname{sgn}(z_n)|z_n|^\delta},
\end{aligned}
$$
since $z_n=-x_ny_n$ and $|y_n|=1$. This is the <signed gluing-map reduction>. If an arbitrary initial value of $y$ is admitted, the formula applies after that first iterate, not before it. The point $z=0$ represents a <separatrix> at which the original flow return is singular, even though the scalar map can be extended continuously there.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let a scalar <fixed point> have magnitude $r>0$. If $z=-r<0$, then $x$ and $y$ have the same sign; the two lifts are **the <fixed points> $(r,1)$ and $(-r,-1)$**. If $z=r>0$, their signs are opposite, and the lift is **the <period-two orbit> $(r,-1)\leftrightarrow(-r,1)$**. The scalar <fixed-point multiplier> is
$$
m=A\delta r^{\delta-1}>0.
$$
The two-return <fixed-point multiplier> of the period-two lift is $m^2$, so in either case <dynamical stability> requires $m<1$. The negative <fixed point> represents one of two mirror-related single-lobe <periodic orbits> in the flow, whereas the positive <fixed point> represents a double-lobe <periodic orbit>.

For $\delta>1$, the nearby <fixed point> is $z=\mu+o(\mu)$ and its <fixed-point multiplier> tends to zero. Thus **two stable single-lobe <limit cycles> for $\mu<0$ glue into one stable double-lobe <limit cycle> for $\mu>0$**. Their periods diverge as the <separatrix> is approached; $\mu=0,z=0$ is not a finite-period <limit cycle>.

For $0<\delta<1$, the small branch instead satisfies $z\sim-\operatorname{sgn}(\mu)(|\mu|/A)^{1/\delta}$ and its <fixed-point multiplier> tends to infinity. Hence **one unstable double-lobe <limit cycle> on $\mu<0$ splits into two unstable single-lobe <limit cycles> on $\mu>0$**. Additional finite-size stable branches depend on the surrounding map; near the resonance $\delta=1$ with $A<1$ they are resolved by the <saddle-node bifurcations> in part (c).

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a <fixed point> write $\mu=h(z)=z-A\operatorname{sgn}(z)|z|^\delta$. A <saddle-node bifurcation> requires both $f(z)=z$ and $f'(z)=1$. For $\delta\ne1$ these conditions give
$$
\boxed{r=(A\delta)^{-1/(\delta-1)},\qquad
z=\pm r,\qquad\mu=\pm\frac{\delta-1}{\delta}r.}
$$
The second derivative is nonzero at either nonzero <saddle-node bifurcation>, making it an ordinary <saddle-node bifurcation>. For $\delta<1$, define
$$
C(\delta)=\frac{1-\delta}{\delta}(A\delta)^{1/(1-\delta)}.
$$
The <saddle-node bifurcations> occur at $(\mu,z)=(-C,r)$ and $(C,-r)$, and **three scalar <fixed points> coexist for $|\mu|<C$**. The two outer branches have <fixed-point multiplier> below one and are stable, while the middle branch is unstable. Negative points lift to two symmetry-related <limit cycles>, so these scalar branch counts need not equal the number of flow <limit cycles>. The cusp records coexistence of distinct return-map branches; even outside it a negative branch represents a symmetry-related pair of flow cycles.

If $\delta=1-\eta$, $\eta\downarrow0$, then
$$
\boxed{C(1-\eta)\sim\frac\eta e A^{1/\eta}.}
$$
This exponentially narrow region ends in a <gluing-map cusp> at $(\mu,\delta)=(0,1)$. At its boundaries the graph of $f$ is tangent to the diagonal at $z=+r$ or $-r$, as shown below. At exactly $\delta=1$, $f=\mu+Az$ and $A<1$ gives one stable <fixed point> for nonzero $\mu$.

For $\delta=1+\eta$, the formal <saddle-node bifurcation> radius is $(A\delta)^{-1/\eta}\to\infty$, and the corresponding <saddle-node bifurcation> parameters also diverge. Thus the near-origin <gluing-map cusp> lies on the $\delta<1$ side; the formal large <saddle-node bifurcations> of the power-law formula on the other side are outside the local gluing-map approximation. They must not be drawn as a second small <gluing-map cusp> near the origin.

\Image[paper-60-gluing.png]
{title=Exponentially narrow gluing-map cusp, tangencies at its two fold boundaries, and stable and unstable fixed-point diagrams on either side of delta one}

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $\delta=1+\varepsilon>1$, the graph of $h(z)$ has a positive maximum at $z=r$ and a negative minimum at $z=-r$. The central branch $|z|<r$ is stable, while the formal outer branches $|z|>r$ are unstable. Near the gluing point only the stable central branch is relevant: it crosses from negative $z$ to positive $z$, representing the stable two-to-one gluing described in part (b). The formal full power-law diagram has three <fixed points> between its two large <saddle-node bifurcations>.

For $\delta=1+\varepsilon<1$, the positive branch has its minimum at $(\mu,z)=(-C,r)$, while the negative branch has its maximum at $(C,-r)$. The inner pieces $|z|<r$ are unstable and outer pieces stable. In the <gluing-map cusp> interval there is a stable negative branch, a stable positive branch, and an unstable central branch; the unstable branch passes through the singular gluing point with opposite sign to $\mu$. Outside the <gluing-map cusp> just one stable outer branch remains. The lower panels of the figure plot these diagrams in fold-scaled variables; dashed lines denote instability and solid lines <dynamical stability>. Neither diagram has a flip bifurcation, since the scalar derivative is positive wherever defined.
